{
  "concept": "An element's position in the periodic table predicts the charge of the ion it forms: main-group metals lose electrons to reach the preceding noble-gas configuration (charge = group number), nonmetals gain electrons (charge = group number − 18), and transition metals break the rule by forming multiple charges.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Title text 'Periodic Table of the Elements' centered at top",
    "Vertical axis label 'Period' in purple, rotated, at far left",
    "Horizontal axis label 'Group' in blue, upper left",
    "Period row numbers 1–7 in purple down the left edge",
    "Group column numbers 1, 2, 3–12, 13–18 in blue above their columns",
    "7 rows × 18 columns grid of square element cells with thin gray borders and rounded corners",
    "Group 1 cells shaded pale purple with labels Li+, Na+, K+, Rb+, Cs+, Fr+ (period 1 group 1 cell is blank/pale gray for H)",
    "Group 2 cells shaded pale green with labels Be2+, Mg2+, Ca2+, Sr2+, Ba2+, Ra2+",
    "d-block groups 3–12 cells shaded pale tan; labeled cells Cr3+/Cr6+, Mn2+, Fe2+/Fe3+, Co2+, Ni2+, Cu+/Cu2+, Zn2+, Ag+, Cd2+, Pt2+, Au+/Au3+, Hg2^2+/Hg2+; remaining d-block cells tan but unlabeled",
    "Group 13–14 cells mostly white with labels Al3+ (period 3, group 13) and C4− (period 2, group 14)",
    "Groups 15–16 nonmetal cells shaded pale pink with labels N3−, O2−, P3−, S2−, As3−, Se2−, Te2−",
    "Group 17 cells shaded pale lavender-blue with labels F−, Cl−, Br−, I−, At−",
    "Group 18 noble gas cells shaded cyan with labels He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, Rn — no charges",
    "Red rectangle outlining the two group-3 cells in periods 6 and 7 (lanthanide/actinide placeholders)",
    "Curved red arrow from the red box down to the detached f-block",
    "Detached f-block: two rows of 14 cells, top row pale green marked '*' (lanthanides), bottom row pale pink marked '**' (actinides), framed by a gray border",
    "Legend mapping fill colors to categories (alkali metals, alkaline earth, transition metals, nonmetals/anions, halogens, noble gases)",
    "Info panel region (right or below) showing selected element symbol, name, ion charge, electron configuration, and the rule sentence",
    "Column highlight overlay band used by the group slider",
    "Annotation callout 'charge = group number' over groups 1–2 and 'charge = group number − 18' over groups 15–17"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "elementClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Why a specific element adopts its charge: the electron configuration it starts from and the noble-gas configuration it reaches by losing or gaining electrons."
    },
    {
      "id": "groupSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Group number",
      "range": [
        1,
        18,
        1
      ],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "That charge is a property of the whole column, not of individual elements — sweeping left to right walks the charge from +1, +2 through the variable d-block to −3, −2, −1, 0.",
      "default_note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "colorScheme",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Color by",
      "options": [
        "Element type",
        "Ion charge"
      ],
      "default": "Element type",
      "teaches": "That the metal/nonmetal classification and the sign-of-charge classification are nearly the same partition of the table — metals become cations, nonmetals become anions — except in the transition block where charge coloring breaks down."
    },
    {
      "id": "ruleTour",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Trend tour",
      "states": [
        "Full table",
        "Cation rule (Groups 1–2, 13)",
        "Anion rule (Groups 15–17)",
        "Noble gas target",
        "Transition metal exceptions"
      ],
      "default": "Full table",
      "teaches": "That one unifying idea — reaching the nearest noble-gas electron count — generates both the cation and anion rules, and that the d-block is where that reasoning stops working."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The table as a charge map",
      "narration": "The periodic table is colored by element family, with each cell carrying the ion that element most commonly forms. Notice that whole columns share one charge.",
      "state": {
        "colorScheme": "Element type",
        "ruleTour": "Full table",
        "groupSweep": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Groups 1 and 2: charge = group number",
      "narration": "Alkali metals lose one electron to give +1; alkaline earths lose two to give +2. Aluminum in Group 13 extends the pattern to +3.",
      "state": {
        "ruleTour": "Cation rule (Groups 1–2, 13)",
        "groupSweep": 2,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Groups 15–17: charge = group − 18",
      "narration": "Nonmetals gain electrons instead. Group 17 gains one for −1, Group 16 gains two for −2, Group 15 gains three for −3 — exactly how far they sit left of the noble gases.",
      "state": {
        "ruleTour": "Anion rule (Groups 15–17)",
        "groupSweep": 17,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why? The noble-gas target",
      "narration": "Group 18 carries no charge label because it already has a full valence shell. Every cation and anion in the table is an atom moving toward the nearest noble-gas configuration.",
      "state": {
        "ruleTour": "Noble gas target",
        "groupSweep": 18,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Where the rule breaks: the d-block",
      "narration": "Transition metals show two or more charges — Cr3+ and Cr6+, Fe2+ and Fe3+, Cu+ and Cu2+ — because d electrons are removed at similar energies. Group position alone cannot predict their charge.",
      "state": {
        "ruleTour": "Transition metal exceptions",
        "groupSweep": 8,
        "colorScheme": "Element type"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two ways to classify the same table",
      "narration": "Recolor by charge value instead of element family: the cation/anion split lands almost exactly on the metal/nonmetal boundary, while the d-block becomes a striped region of ambiguity.",
      "state": {
        "colorScheme": "Ion charge",
        "ruleTour": "Full table",
        "groupSweep": 8
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Ionic charge isn't memorized element by element — it's read off the column: every atom gains or loses the fewest electrons needed to match the nearest noble gas, which is why charge equals the group number for metals and group-number-minus-18 for nonmetals, and why the d-block, with no single nearby noble gas, refuses to obey.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate a wide 18-column, 7-row periodic-table grid of rounded squares on white, with the detached 14-wide f-block below and right of center, framed in gray and linked by a curved red arrow from a red-outlined box around the group-3 cells of periods 6 and 7. Color fills are pastel: Group 1 pale purple, Group 2 pale green, d-block pale tan, Groups 15–16 nonmetals pale pink, Group 17 pale lavender-blue, Group 18 cyan, unfilled/metalloid cells white. Element symbols are bold black with superscripted charges (e.g. Li+, O2−, Fe2+/Fe3+ stacked on two lines). Group numbers in blue above the columns (1, 2, then 3–12 above the d-block, then 13–18), period numbers 1–7 in purple at left, with rotated 'Period' and 'Group' axis labels in matching colors. Reserve a right-hand or lower info panel for the clicked element's symbol, name, charge, electron configuration (e.g. Na: [Ne]3s1 → Na+: [Ne]) and a one-line rule sentence. The group slider draws a translucent yellow column band with a caption naming the typical charge for that group; the charge toggle recolors cells on a diverging blue(negative)–gray(zero)–red(positive) scale with the d-block hatched to signal variability. Trend-tour states dim non-relevant cells to ~20% opacity and reveal the annotation callouts 'charge = group number' and 'charge = group number − 18'."
}

